; [269] { ---- Load ---------------------------------------------------------------- }
; [270] 
; [271] { Everything ZMemInit can reject on the strength of the first 64 bytes, done
; [272]   separately and SILENTLY so the caller can decide whether it is going to run
; [273]   before it registers as a shell.
; [274] 
; [275]   The reason is k/OS's, not the Z-machine's. sys_register_shell allocates a
; [276]   back-buffer and leaves the task backgrounded; from then on our output is
; [277]   recorded, not shown, and if we Halt, _ReapDeadTask frees that buffer and
; [278]   repaints from the incoming shell. A diagnostic written after registering is
; [279]   destroyed in the same instant it becomes true. So the checks that a user can
; [280]   actually provoke -- no such file, not a v3 story, a story larger than this
; [281]   run's pages -- happen HERE, before registration, where WriteLn still reaches
; [282]   the terminal; and the load's progress output happens after it, where it
; [283]   belongs in the back-buffer and is waiting when you switch to us.
; [284] 
; [285]   Of ZMemInit's failures only two need the read loop -- a MemRead hard error,
; [286]   and a header claiming more bytes than the file holds. Both are I/O faults
; [287]   rather than user error, and the caller reports them by exit code alone.
; [288] 
; [289]   Cheap, and free of consequence: the 64 bytes land at story offset 0 on ZPg0,
; [290]   which ZMemInit's own read overwrites from byte 0 a moment later, and every
; [291]   header variable this sets ZParseHeader sets again. The file is opened twice.
; [292] 
; [293]   The page arithmetic is repeated rather than shared. It is three lines, it
; [294]   must be right in ZMemInit whether or not anyone probed first, and a version
; [295]   of it that only ran on one of the two paths is the kind of thing that is
; [296]   correct until the day the probe is skipped. }
