; [369] { ---- The encoder ---------------------------------------------------------
; [370] 
; [371]   The inverse of ZTDecode, and it lives here rather than in zdict so that the
; [372]   alphabet table has exactly one definition. Two copies would disagree inside
; [373]   six months, which is the same argument that keeps one decoder serving both
; [374]   output sinks.
; [375] 
; [376]   A v3 dictionary word is SIX z-chars in two words, the second with bit 15
; [377]   set, padded with z-char 5. Verified against all 684 entries of Zork I
; [378]   release 119: every one re-encodes byte-exact, including `$ve' (the ZSCII
; [379]   escape), `.' (bare A2 punctuation) and `air-p' (truncated mid-word).
; [380] 
; [381]   TRUNCATION CAN CUT AN ESCAPE IN HALF. A 10-bit ZSCII escape is four z-chars;
; [382]   if only two slots remain, two of them are simply dropped. That is legal and
; [383]   Infocom relied on it, which is why z-chars are pushed ONE AT A TIME through
; [384]   a bounds-checked helper instead of being assembled in groups and appended.
