; [63]   (* Diagnose BEFORE registering as a shell; do the WORK after.
; [64] 
; [65]      sys_register_shell allocates a back-buffer, splices this task into the
; [66]      shell ring after kosh, and leaves it BACKGROUNDED. From that moment our
; [67]      console output is recorded only in OUR back-buffer -- and if we then
; [68]      Halt, _ReapDeadTask unsplices us, FREES that buffer, and repaints the
; [69]      screen from the incoming foreground shell's buffer instead. A startup
; [70]      message written after RegisterShell is therefore destroyed in the same
; [71]      instant the task exits: the usage line below was invisible for exactly
; [72]      this reason.
; [73] 
; [74]      Before registration we are an ordinary task with no back-buffer, so
; [75]      WriteLn goes straight to the terminal and kosh -- still FOREGROUND_TCB,
; [76]      blocked in sys_wait -- never repaints over it. The message survives, the
; [77]      way any plain .COM's output does.
; [78] 
; [79]      That argues for registering late, and for a while the whole load sat in
; [80]      front of it. The cost showed up under `zork zork1 &': kosh prints [bg N],
; [81]      returns to its REPL and paints its prompt, and then the load's Loading
; [82]      and Preparing lines arrive on top of it from a task that is supposed to
; [83]      be in the background. They reach the terminal precisely BECAUSE we have
; [84]      not registered yet -- output from a task with no back-buffer takes the
; [85]      fast path to the terminal MMIO whether it is foreground or not.
; [86] 
; [87]      So the line is not drawn at the load, it is drawn at DIAGNOSIS. ZMemProbe
; [88]      answers everything a user can get wrong -- no such file, not a v3 story,
; [89]      too big for our pages -- from 64 bytes and in silence. Those reports stay
; [90]      out here where they can be read. Registration follows. The load, the
; [91]      checksum and the banner all happen inside our back-buffer, and `&' is
; [92]      silent as it should be.
; [93] 
; [94]      What is left over is the read loop's own two failures, a MemRead hard
; [95]      error and a header longer than the file. They are I/O faults, not typing
; [96]      mistakes, they cannot be seen from the header, and by then we are a
